 ## Background
-CodecGS was introduced as a method to compress 3D Gaussian Splatting by employing optimized feature planes and standard video codecs [S1]. It builds on a progressive training strategy that captures coarse geometric information initially and gradually adds finer details [S1]. The method is implemented in the `gsplat` library, an open-source library for CUDA-accelerated differentiable rasterization of 3D Gaussians [S1].
+CodecGS was introduced as a method to compress 3D Gaussian Splatting by employing optimized feature planes and standard video codecs [S1]. It builds on a progressive training strategy that captures coarse geometric information initially and gradually adds finer details [S1]. The method introduces progressive tri-planes where the tri-plane takes 3D point positions $\mathbf{x}$ as input and predicts the corresponding attributes for each point [S1]. A two-phase training, starting with standard 3DGS training followed by feature plane training, ensures densification is consistent [S1]. Progressive training addresses the instability of feature plane training due to sparse 3DGS signals [S1]. DCT entropy modeling is employed to transform the feature planes [S1]. After training, feature planes are normalized and converted to 16-bit integers, corresponding to the YUV 16-bit format, and are encoded by a standard video codec [S1].
 
